+# VetrOFF fork history (separate from upstream)
+
+## XHTTP: cancellation while a response is pending
+
+Commit: `018d3d00563e2addad14cb20e21dd5b54e962ddb`
+
+**What happened:** a canceled OpenStream returned a nil reader while its HTTP goroutine still referenced the named return value. A late HTTP error dereferenced nil and crashed the process.
+
+**Fix:** retain the concrete response reader in the HTTP goroutine. A deterministic canceled-request regression reproduces the panic before the fix; the XHTTP package passes five race runs afterwards.
+
+---
+
+## WARP: preserve explicit fallback addresses for existing consumers
+
+Commit: `bf0c1b6e6fb004bdef85c066101ff455d744898e`
+
+**What happened:** synchronization removed `WARPEndpointOptions.FallbackAddress`. Existing `fallback_address` configs failed strict decoding and hiddify-core's WARP-in-WARP policy no longer compiled.
+
+**Fix:** restore the optional IP/list field and pass it to the WireGuard/AmneziaWG tunnel options. The strict-decoding regression fails before the fix and passes five race runs afterwards. Existing profiles keep their explicit fallback policy. Regenerate the schema against the pinned dependencies, including their canonical user field names.
+
+---
+
+## Device and cached connections: safe address changes and close
+
+Commit: `55322481164badafade6babea5552e9f238b7862`
+
+**What happened:** stack UDP sockets retained obsolete local addresses after reconfiguration. Concurrent cached-connection reads and close could release the same buffer twice. AWG netstack close could close a channel while a packet writer used it, or close the channel twice.
+
+**Fix:** import upstream v5 `6a545ead13f09130c7c802fa948b9d22f33c5000`: interrupt UDP sockets only for the changed address family, update AWG to `a2ae165a`, and use `hiddify/sing` at `e1f78e79` through a submodule and replacements in both Go modules. Preserve the fork's stack ownership callbacks. Regression tests reproduce all three failures on the previous code/pins and pass after the update, including repeated race runs. CI now continuously covers these packages and reruns official Snell integration when the sing dependency changes.
+
+---
+
+## XHTTP: obfuscated padding and REALITY auto mode after synchronization
+
+Commit: `2c572649b644b68da8bd430ce95bf42f0b581e74`, `834ae4f18f10f2f3c2262a08b2459edd46e83ccb`
+
+**What happened:** Xray latest and 26.3.27 clients using header padding were rejected with HTTP 400. A sing-box client in `auto` mode used packet-up over REALITY, so stream-one-only Xray servers timed out.
+
+**Fix:** validate padding through the existing placement-aware extractor and method-aware validator. Resolve client auto mode before creating session metadata: stream-one for REALITY, stream-up for REALITY with a separate download, packet-up otherwise. A server regression fails before the fix, and the full XHTTP package passes afterwards. The generated schema also includes the restored Bond outbound.
+
+
+**Race coverage:** synchronize response-body publication and close, and record upload-buffer lengths before transferring ownership. The full XHTTP race suite passed five consecutive runs; this package is now included in CI race regressions.
+
+---
+
+## Bond: outbound registration lost during synchronization
+
+Commit: `a3d00f565c9095ffc7105cd568b995d87f96b18c`
+
+**What happened:** production-tag configurations failed to load with `unknown outbound type: bond`; the existing registration helper was no longer called.
+
+**Fix:** call the Bond outbound registration helper, provide its build-disabled stub, and test the outbound option registry. The existing end-to-end Bond test reproduced the failure in CI.
+
+---
+
+## URL tests: a sub-millisecond success looked unmeasured
+
+Commit: `4539248b54962e054088efc6f71383ac68208f5d`
+
+**What happened:** truncating a successful probe to milliseconds produced zero for fast replies. Monitoring reserves zero for an unmeasured result, so staged checks retried a working proxy and selection tests became unstable.
+
+**Fix:** successful regular and unified probes return at least 1 ms. A regression test exercises both paths with a local HTTP server; it fails before the fix. The remaining multi-platform formatting and promoted-field initializer warnings are repaired without disabling linters.
+
+---
+
+## Synchronization regressions: URL stages and MASQUE address families
+
+Commit: `df7ada19d2cd46e8dfab351fac990deb268b6087`
+
+**What happened:** a new monitoring URL stage could join a test that had already settled and reuse the previous URL's failure. MASQUE's legacy stack bound IPv6 UDP to the IPv4 address and did not unwrap mapped IPv4 destinations. New DNS discovery adds candidates beyond the bundled list, so the old exact-count test no longer described auto mode.
+
+**Fix:** publish each test's completion before its stage result, with completion owned by that execution; bind each address family correctly and unwrap mapped IPv4. Regression tests failed before these fixes and pass afterwards. Resolver tests assert all bundled candidates plus correctly classified discovery candidates. Keep the fork's explicit registration detour, and keep WARP handshakes fresh with keepalive.
+
+---
+
+## Go 1.27 and synchronization compatibility
+
+Commit: `e7ea0260` (options, races and schema); TCP Fast Open dependency updated in this synchronization.
+
+**What happened:** upstream configs used `finalmask` while the fork accepted `final_mask`; concurrent monitoring read mutable histories and IP-provider defaults; the schema generator rejected the fork's CIDR tag. The old `tfo-go` called Go 1.27's `net.newFD` using its previous return signature, producing invalid errors in Windows hot-reload traffic tests.
+
+**Fix:** retain both mask keys (legacy precedence), publish immutable history snapshots, keep default User-Agent local, support string-backed `prefixable` fields, and upgrade `tfo-go` to `v2.4.2`. XDrive stays registered in ordinary builds and its integration fixtures use encrypted VLESS. Explicit debug panics preserve vet compatibility.
+
+---
+
+## Fork synchronization: retain queue rollback and XDrive configuration compatibility
+
+Commit: `5ab27d15`
+
+**What happened:** upstream's monitoring queue did not restore its old state when full. Replacing the fork's XDrive copy also changed the names of fronting fields and the template representation.
+
+**Fix:** retain bounded-queue rollback and its regression checks; normalize old XDrive template strings and fronting names, preserve storage routing, and close active storage connections when their transport closes. The standalone smart DNS service remains registered for existing configurations. The synchronization tracks upstream `2f43880d30e1f4d71154b0c9d3fa309c4ece4b26`.
+
+---
+
+## CI: restore compilation and regression coverage
+
+Commit: `a045a8c9678ffff904be367a72136a34b08294b9`
+
+**What happened:** Tests called `Status()` on the upstream outbound interface, GeoIP export still built the old CIDR representation, and libbox contained duplicated report/log functions, missing DNS monitor fields and stale platform references. DNSCrypt certificate queries acquired TCP length framing through wrapped UDP connections. QUIC handshake errors could incorrectly permit fallback after certificate verification failed. Closing WireGuard or AmneziaWG could wait indefinitely for a stalled detour write. Several tests still expected behaviour that earlier fixes had replaced.
+
+**Fix:** Use the outbound status adapter and string CIDRs, restore the libbox monitor state and remove duplicate/stale code. Preserve the packet-connection interface for DNSCrypt. Classify HTTP/3 acquisition and stream errors before allowing fallback. Close tunnel binds before waiting for device locks. Make the AmneziaWG regression wait for its deferred bind before explicitly initiating a handshake, update obsolete assertions, and re-enable the fixed noise layout/count, DNS metadata and round-robin overflow tests. Record missing module requirements and checksums so CI does not resolve new versions on each run.
+
+## Power report: macOS build failure and truncated network counters
+
+Commit: `146b764703defe38a2279caf17c77d648addae8a`
+
+**What happened:** On macOS the network interface reader produced `uint64` byte and packet counts, but the shared structure accepted only `uint32`, stopping compilation. Linux discarded the upper 32 bits of counters, making traffic totals wrap after 4 GiB.
+
+**Fix:** Store 64-bit counters on all platforms, preserve Linux and macOS values, and ignore counter resets when computing interval deltas instead of reporting huge values. Regression tests cover large counters and resets.
+
+## Build: v5 outbound status and binary rule-set recovery did not compile
+
+Commit: `bdc860ba937a1c6a983cbb99bed5ef0710a42898`
+
+**What happened:** The fork called `Status()` on every `adapter.Outbound` even though the upstream interface only guarantees `IsReady()`. Recovering CIDR rules from SRS also assigned `[]*Prefixable` to the source JSON fields, which now require `Listable[string]`. An unused import in the inbound adapter broke compilation as well.
+
+**Fix:** Prefer the detailed `Status()` method when an outbound implements it, and fall back to `IsReady()` for standard v5 outbounds. Recover CIDR fields as strings. Remove the unused import and add regression tests for the status fallback and CIDR recovery.
+
+## Latest v5 additions in this fork
+
+The synchronization includes Soar and resolver discovery, joined monitoring tests, stable balancer selection and WARP/MASQUE refusal recovery. The fork retains the `IsReady` adapter contract; detailed `Status` and `StatusError` are optional capabilities. Legacy `pool_tolerance` is accepted when `tolerance` is unset.
+
+Standalone `masque` now requires an enrolled `device`; use a `warp` endpoint with `mode: masque` for automatic registration. The app should display the separate status and error fields instead of expecting status marks in the display type.
